 ## Overview
-This method accelerates direct volume rendering by storing volumetric data in 3D texture memory and using dedicated texture mapping hardware to resample the volume along textured polygons [S1][S2, unofficial]. It solves the performance bottleneck of software-based ray casting by offloading trilinear interpolation and sample reconstruction to parallel hardware units, enabling interactive frame rates for large datasets [S4]. Unlike [Shear-Warp](link-to-shear-warp), which performs classification before bilinear resampling and requires a final warp step, the Planar Texture Resampling variant of this method performs trilinear interpolation followed by classification and requires no final warp, resulting in higher image quality and consistent performance for perspective projections [S1].
+This method accelerates direct volume ray casting by storing the volume in 3D texture memory and resampling it during rendering by extracting textured planes parallel to the image plane [S1][S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of high-performance reconstruction and resampling by leveraging dedicated 3D texturing hardware (specifically the Silicon Graphics RealityEngine) to perform trilinear interpolation on polygons embedded in the texture, offloading the geometric sampling from the CPU to the graphics pipeline [S2, unofficial]. Unlike [Shear-Warp](5616-hardware-accelerated-volume-rendering.md), which performs classification before bilinear resampling and requires a final warp step, the Planes method performs trilinear interpolation followed by classification and requires no final warp, resulting in higher image quality and consistent performance for perspective projections [S1].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** A scalar volume dataset (density/attenuation), a viewing matrix, and optional precomputed shading coefficients.
-*   **Output:** A 2D image in the frame buffer with color and opacity values.
